1
00:00:00,341 --> 00:00:02,667
Last video ended here, with the sine curve,

2
00:00:03,108 --> 00:00:04,010
x in radians.

3
00:00:04,893 --> 00:00:06,509
Here's where a sine hump turns up.

4
00:00:07,134 --> 00:00:09,181
A home's solar panels make no power at

5
00:00:09,222 --> 00:00:11,870
sunrise, the most at noon, and none again

6
00:00:11,951 --> 00:00:12,613
at sunset.

7
00:00:13,396 --> 00:00:15,702
The energy they've collected keeps building all day,

8
00:00:16,064 --> 00:00:16,945
fastest at noon.

9
00:00:17,769 --> 00:00:19,214
Its gradient is the power,

10
00:00:19,717 --> 00:00:21,804
so the energy curve is a primitive of

11
00:00:21,906 --> 00:00:22,547
a sine curve.

12
00:00:23,653 --> 00:00:25,780
So which curve has gradient sine x?

13
00:00:26,627 --> 00:00:27,918
Cos x is the obvious guess.

14
00:00:28,747 --> 00:00:30,121
Test it at pi over two.

15
00:00:30,907 --> 00:00:33,015
Sine x is one there, so our curve

16
00:00:33,036 --> 00:00:34,823
should be climbing, with gradient one.

17
00:00:35,466 --> 00:00:38,039
But cos x is falling there, with gradient

18
00:00:38,181 --> 00:00:38,764
minus one.

19
00:00:39,408 --> 00:00:40,696
The guess has the wrong sign.

20
00:00:41,705 --> 00:00:43,276
So where does the minus sign go?

21
00:00:43,931 --> 00:00:44,561
Check another point.

22
00:00:45,108 --> 00:00:47,976
At three pi over two, sine x is

23
00:00:48,137 --> 00:00:50,865
minus one, but cos x is climbing there,

24
00:00:51,067 --> 00:00:51,929
with gradient one.

25
00:00:53,052 --> 00:00:55,436
Everywhere, the gradient of cos x is minus

26
00:00:55,578 --> 00:00:57,641
sine x, the exact opposite of what we

27
00:00:57,682 --> 00:00:57,842
need.

28
00:00:58,628 --> 00:01:00,601
So turn cos x upside down.

29
00:01:01,145 --> 00:01:03,610
Flipping a curve upside down reverses every slope,

30
00:01:03,852 --> 00:01:04,573
at the same x.

31
00:01:05,656 --> 00:01:08,299
Now minus cos x climbs from zero to

32
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:08,760
pi,

33
00:01:09,402 --> 00:01:12,165
exactly where sine x is positive, and falls

34
00:01:12,306 --> 00:01:14,288
from pi to two pi, where sine x

35
00:01:14,409 --> 00:01:14,909
is negative.

36
00:01:16,054 --> 00:01:18,264
Watch the gradient of minus cos x as

37
00:01:18,285 --> 00:01:19,409
a point moves along.

38
00:01:20,053 --> 00:01:21,741
It traces sine x exactly.

39
00:01:22,604 --> 00:01:26,029
Differentiate back: minus cos x has derivative minus,

40
00:01:26,351 --> 00:01:28,333
minus sine x, which is sine x.

41
00:01:28,936 --> 00:01:30,881
So the integral of sine x is minus

42
00:01:30,922 --> 00:01:32,225
cos x, plus C.

43
00:01:32,861 --> 00:01:34,390
Cos x is the easier one.

44
00:01:34,933 --> 00:01:37,345
From the derivative rules, the gradient of sine

45
00:01:37,405 --> 00:01:38,491
x is cos x.

46
00:01:39,295 --> 00:01:41,841
At zero, sine x climbs with gradient one,

47
00:01:42,263 --> 00:01:43,605
and cos of zero is one.

48
00:01:44,329 --> 00:01:47,040
At pi over two it's flat, and cos

49
00:01:47,062 --> 00:01:48,427
of pi over two is zero.

50
00:01:49,211 --> 00:01:51,456
So the integral of cos x is sine

51
00:01:51,497 --> 00:01:52,719
x, plus C,

52
00:01:53,221 --> 00:01:54,282
with no minus sign.

53
00:01:55,206 --> 00:01:57,651
Both rules come from derivatives that only work

54
00:01:57,712 --> 00:02:00,678
with x in radians, so these primitives need

55
00:02:00,739 --> 00:02:01,399
radians too.

56
00:02:02,021 --> 00:02:04,068
The third rule comes from tan x.

57
00:02:04,992 --> 00:02:07,118
In an earlier video, the derivative of tan

58
00:02:07,160 --> 00:02:09,527
x was sec squared x, which means one

59
00:02:09,569 --> 00:02:10,471
over cos squared x.

60
00:02:11,114 --> 00:02:13,345
So the integral of sec squared x is

61
00:02:13,425 --> 00:02:14,631
tan x, plus C.

62
00:02:15,757 --> 00:02:16,321
Look at the graphs.

63
00:02:17,007 --> 00:02:19,153
Cos squared x is at most one,

64
00:02:19,735 --> 00:02:21,801
so sec squared x is never less than

65
00:02:21,921 --> 00:02:22,021
one,

66
00:02:22,663 --> 00:02:24,950
and tan x climbs all along each piece.

67
00:02:26,154 --> 00:02:28,622
At pi over four, its gradient is two,

68
00:02:29,265 --> 00:02:31,452
and sec squared of pi over four is

69
00:02:31,694 --> 00:02:31,914
two.

70
00:02:32,818 --> 00:02:35,308
But tan x has asymptotes, wherever cos x

71
00:02:35,409 --> 00:02:37,376
is zero, such as at pi over two.

72
00:02:38,119 --> 00:02:40,466
So, like the natural log of mod x

73
00:02:40,568 --> 00:02:43,216
last video, the answer works on one piece

74
00:02:43,277 --> 00:02:43,718
at a time.

75
00:02:44,529 --> 00:02:45,755
Now put an inside in.

76
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:49,395
Find the integral of fifteen cos five x.

77
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:51,172
Try sine five x.

78
00:02:52,021 --> 00:02:55,009
On the graph, that's sine x squashed sideways

79
00:02:55,070 --> 00:02:57,678
by a factor of five, so every slope

80
00:02:57,819 --> 00:02:58,901
is multiplied by five.

81
00:02:59,785 --> 00:03:02,354
Its derivative is five cos five x.

82
00:03:03,017 --> 00:03:05,542
We need fifteen cos five x, which is

83
00:03:05,644 --> 00:03:06,645
three times as much.

84
00:03:07,529 --> 00:03:10,075
So the answer is three sine five x,

85
00:03:10,618 --> 00:03:10,938
plus C.

86
00:03:11,922 --> 00:03:12,684
Differentiate back:

87
00:03:13,227 --> 00:03:16,397
three times five cos five x is fifteen

88
00:03:16,518 --> 00:03:17,320
cos five x.

89
00:03:18,164 --> 00:03:19,709
It's the same cause as the last two

90
00:03:19,771 --> 00:03:20,131
videos.

91
00:03:20,774 --> 00:03:23,479
A linear inside multiplies the gradient by its

92
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:23,820
rate,

93
00:03:24,342 --> 00:03:26,205
so the primitive divides by it.

94
00:03:27,135 --> 00:03:28,060
Back to the solar panels.

95
00:03:28,645 --> 00:03:31,750
With t in hours after sunrise, their power

96
00:03:31,871 --> 00:03:34,616
is five sine of pi t over twelve

97
00:03:34,677 --> 00:03:35,177
kilowatts.

98
00:03:35,879 --> 00:03:38,166
The inner rate is pi over twelve, so

99
00:03:38,227 --> 00:03:38,828
divide by it.

100
00:03:39,411 --> 00:03:42,482
The energy is minus sixty over pi, times

101
00:03:42,563 --> 00:03:44,651
cos of pi t over twelve, plus C.

102
00:03:45,716 --> 00:03:48,064
At sunrise the energy is zero, and cos

103
00:03:48,085 --> 00:03:49,569
of zero is one, so C

104
00:03:50,092 --> 00:03:51,055
is sixty over pi.

105
00:03:51,719 --> 00:03:54,396
By noon, that's about nineteen kilowatt hours.

106
00:03:54,920 --> 00:03:57,269
By sunset, double that, about thirty-eight.

107
00:03:57,914 --> 00:04:00,089
Half the day's energy comes before noon.

108
00:04:00,881 --> 00:04:03,634
Next, the integral of two sec squared, of

109
00:04:03,754 --> 00:04:05,622
two x minus pi over three.

110
00:04:06,405 --> 00:04:08,551
The inside has rate two, and the two

111
00:04:08,611 --> 00:04:09,594
in front matches it.

112
00:04:10,075 --> 00:04:12,161
So the answer is just tan of two

113
00:04:12,301 --> 00:04:14,487
x minus pi over three, plus C.

114
00:04:15,350 --> 00:04:15,611
Check:

115
00:04:16,154 --> 00:04:18,263
its derivative is two sec squared of two

116
00:04:18,383 --> 00:04:19,488
x minus pi over three.

117
00:04:20,332 --> 00:04:22,257
The minus pi over three is a slide,

118
00:04:22,579 --> 00:04:24,042
and a slide changes no slopes.

119
00:04:24,748 --> 00:04:25,578
Keep the asymptotes.

120
00:04:26,025 --> 00:04:27,893
They're where two x minus pi over three

121
00:04:28,014 --> 00:04:30,565
equals pi over two, plus any multiple of

122
00:04:30,626 --> 00:04:30,906
pi.

123
00:04:31,408 --> 00:04:33,594
That's at x equals five pi over twelve,

124
00:04:33,955 --> 00:04:35,699
and every pi over two from there.

125
00:04:36,309 --> 00:04:38,323
What if the inside isn't linear?

126
00:04:39,008 --> 00:04:41,734
Find the integral of minus two x, times

127
00:04:41,836 --> 00:04:43,539
sine of x squared plus one.

128
00:04:44,323 --> 00:04:46,230
The inside is x squared plus one,

129
00:04:46,733 --> 00:04:49,021
and its derivative, two x, is out the

130
00:04:49,062 --> 00:04:49,242
front.

131
00:04:50,066 --> 00:04:52,412
And minus sine is the derivative of cos.

132
00:04:52,995 --> 00:04:55,102
So this is the chain rule in reverse,

133
00:04:55,645 --> 00:04:57,833
and the answer is cos of x squared

134
00:04:57,854 --> 00:04:59,017
plus one, plus C.

135
00:04:59,842 --> 00:05:00,644
Differentiate back:

136
00:05:01,207 --> 00:05:03,776
minus sine of x squared plus one, times

137
00:05:03,878 --> 00:05:04,278
two x.

138
00:05:05,004 --> 00:05:05,893
That's what we started with.

139
00:05:06,683 --> 00:05:08,700
The inner derivative has to be there.

140
00:05:09,385 --> 00:05:11,392
Cos of x squared, on its own, has

141
00:05:11,513 --> 00:05:13,720
no two x out the front, so these

142
00:05:13,802 --> 00:05:14,865
rules can't integrate it.

143
00:05:15,495 --> 00:05:17,988
The same reverse chain rule works for cos

144
00:05:18,170 --> 00:05:19,174
and sec squared.

145
00:05:19,999 --> 00:05:22,609
Find the integral of three x squared, times

146
00:05:22,730 --> 00:05:23,914
cos of x cubed.

147
00:05:24,618 --> 00:05:27,103
The inside is x cubed, and three x

148
00:05:27,144 --> 00:05:29,188
squared is its derivative, out the front.

149
00:05:29,790 --> 00:05:31,856
So the answer is sine of x cubed,

150
00:05:32,017 --> 00:05:32,438
plus C.

151
00:05:33,421 --> 00:05:34,543
Now a harder-looking one:

152
00:05:35,146 --> 00:05:37,871
cos x, times sec squared of sine x.

153
00:05:38,654 --> 00:05:41,321
The inside is sine x, and its derivative,

154
00:05:41,442 --> 00:05:42,665
cos x, is out the front,

155
00:05:43,188 --> 00:05:45,253
so the answer is tan of sine x,

156
00:05:45,394 --> 00:05:45,794
plus C.

157
00:05:46,701 --> 00:05:47,733
What about the asymptotes?

158
00:05:48,521 --> 00:05:51,134
Tan needs its inside to avoid pi over

159
00:05:51,195 --> 00:05:51,435
two.

160
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:54,403
But sine x stays between minus one and

161
00:05:54,584 --> 00:05:54,704
one,

162
00:05:55,326 --> 00:05:57,148
and pi over two is about one point

163
00:05:57,209 --> 00:05:57,749
five seven,

164
00:05:58,271 --> 00:05:59,031
so it never gets there.

165
00:05:59,676 --> 00:06:01,310
This answer works for every x.

166
00:06:01,882 --> 00:06:03,391
Here's a way to remember the signs.

167
00:06:04,132 --> 00:06:07,235
Differentiating moves one step forward around this wheel:

168
00:06:07,795 --> 00:06:11,718
sine, cos, minus sine, minus cos, and back

169
00:06:11,738 --> 00:06:12,099
to sine.

170
00:06:13,042 --> 00:06:14,931
Integrating moves one step back.

171
00:06:15,634 --> 00:06:17,863
One step back from sine is minus cos.

172
00:06:18,527 --> 00:06:20,326
One step back from cos is sine.

173
00:06:21,131 --> 00:06:22,558
Sec squared isn't on the wheel.

174
00:06:22,820 --> 00:06:23,593
It comes from tan.

175
00:06:26,303 --> 00:06:26,708
Your turn.

176
00:06:27,253 --> 00:06:29,983
Find the integral of six cos three x.

177
00:06:30,667 --> 00:06:31,496
Pause here to answer.

178
00:06:42,248 --> 00:06:45,237
Sine three x has derivative three cos three

179
00:06:45,378 --> 00:06:45,518
x.

180
00:06:46,141 --> 00:06:48,188
We need six cos three x, which is

181
00:06:48,269 --> 00:06:48,730
twice that.

182
00:06:49,354 --> 00:06:51,708
So the answer is two sine three x,

183
00:06:52,071 --> 00:06:52,432
plus C.

184
00:06:53,337 --> 00:06:54,198
Differentiate back:

185
00:06:54,701 --> 00:06:57,246
two times three cos three x is six

186
00:06:57,327 --> 00:06:58,088
cos three x.

187
00:06:58,912 --> 00:07:00,802
Eighteen sine three x is wrong.

188
00:07:01,265 --> 00:07:03,500
It multiplies by the inner three, when it

189
00:07:03,541 --> 00:07:04,084
should divide.

190
00:07:05,747 --> 00:07:06,135
Last one.

191
00:07:07,025 --> 00:07:10,240
Find the integral of four x, times sine

192
00:07:10,381 --> 00:07:11,205
of x squared.

193
00:07:12,092 --> 00:07:12,837
Pause here to answer.

194
00:07:23,521 --> 00:07:26,213
The inside is x squared, with derivative two

195
00:07:26,393 --> 00:07:26,514
x,

196
00:07:27,016 --> 00:07:28,462
and four x is twice that.

197
00:07:29,266 --> 00:07:31,602
One step back from sine is minus cos.

198
00:07:32,126 --> 00:07:34,354
So the answer is minus two cos of

199
00:07:34,474 --> 00:07:35,638
x squared, plus C.

200
00:07:36,501 --> 00:07:37,322
Differentiate back:

201
00:07:37,883 --> 00:07:40,528
minus two, times minus sine of x squared,

202
00:07:40,868 --> 00:07:43,754
times two x, gives four x sine of

203
00:07:43,994 --> 00:07:44,495
x squared.

204
00:07:45,259 --> 00:07:46,897
Two cos of x squared is wrong.

205
00:07:47,382 --> 00:07:48,997
Its derivative has a minus sign.

206
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:54,103
So each trig primitive undoes a derivative rule,

207
00:07:54,323 --> 00:07:56,825
with its sign, its inner rate and its

208
00:07:56,885 --> 00:07:57,286
plus C.

209
00:07:58,149 --> 00:07:59,301
That's the last of the primitives.

210
00:08:00,130 --> 00:08:01,544
Next, a new question.

211
00:08:02,250 --> 00:08:03,012
Here's cos x,

212
00:08:03,553 --> 00:08:05,318
from zero to pi over two,

213
00:08:05,839 --> 00:08:08,085
with thin rectangles standing against it.

214
00:08:08,847 --> 00:08:10,311
What happens when we add them up,

215
00:08:10,814 --> 00:08:12,439
and make them thinner and thinner?

216
00:08:15,155 --> 00:08:16,038
Here are the key notes.

217
00:08:16,561 --> 00:08:18,329
Pause the video if you need more time

218
00:08:18,350 --> 00:08:18,831
to copy them.
